{"graph_chars": "When an event hits a market, use four steps. One: draw demand and supply before the event and mark the old equilibrium. Two: does the event hit demand or supply? Three: does the curve shift right or left? Draw it. Four: compare the new equilibrium price and quantity with the old. Salmon, California, two thousand fifteen. Excellent weather, more plankton, calm seas. That is a natural condition, so supply. Supply shifts right. Price falls from three dollars twenty-five a pound to two dollars fifty. Quantity rises from two hundred fifty thousand fish to five hundred fifty thousand. Demand did not move. Quantity demanded rose because we slid down the demand curve. Print news: tastes shift to digital, demand for print shifts left, both price and quantity fall. When both curves move, as with the Postal Service \u2014 higher worker pay shifts supply left, email shifts demand left \u2014 quantity definitely falls. Price is ambiguous unless you know which shift is larger.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0649], [0.0649, 0.1298], [0.1298, 0.1947], [0.1947, 0.2596], [0.2596, 0.3245], [0.3245, 0.3894], [0.3894, 0.4543], [0.4543, 0.5192], [0.5192, 0.5841], [0.5841, 0.649], [0.649, 0.7139], [0.7139, 0.7788], [0.7788, 0.8437], [0.8437, 0.9086], [0.9086, 0.9735], [0.9735, 1.0384], [1.0384, 1.1033], [1.1033, 1.1682], [1.1682, 1.2331], [1.2331, 1.298], [1.298, 1.3629], [1.3629, 1.4278], [1.4278, 1.4927], [1.4927, 1.5576], [1.5576, 1.6225], [1.6225, 1.6874], [1.6874, 1.7523], [1.7523, 1.8172], [1.8172, 1.8822], [1.8822, 1.9471], [1.9471, 2.012], [2.012, 2.0769], [2.0769, 2.1418], [2.1418, 2.2067], [2.2067, 2.2716], [2.2716, 2.3365], [2.3365, 2.4014], [2.4014, 2.4663], [2.4663, 2.5312], [2.5312, 2.5961], [2.5961, 2.661], [2.661, 2.7259], [2.7259, 2.7908], [2.7908, 2.8557], [2.8557, 2.9206], [2.9206, 2.9855], [2.9855, 3.0504], [3.0504, 3.1153], [3.1153, 3.1802], [3.1802, 3.2451], [3.2451, 3.31], [3.31, 3.3749], [3.3749, 3.4398], [3.4398, 3.5047], [3.5047, 3.5696], [3.5696, 3.6